              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "p47-ep-17-short-method",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "fontSize": null,
            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 0,
            "spaceAfter": 2,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Set ",
                "marks": []
              },
              {
                "type": "math",
                "latex": "y'=6x(x^2-1)^2=0",
                "display": false
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ", then substitute each x-value into the curve.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "worked": "\n\n$$\\begin{align*}y'&=3(x^2-1)^2(2x)\\\\y'&=6x(x^2-1)^2.\\end{align*}$$\n\n A horizontal tangent has gradient $0$, so\n\n\n\n$$\\begin{align*}6x(x^2-1)^2&=0\\\\x&=-1,\\ 0,\\ 1.\\end{align*}$$\n\n\n\nSubstitution in $y=(x^2-1)^3$ gives $y=0,-1,0$, respectively. The points are $(-1,0)$, $(0,-1)$ and $(1,0)$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
